---
layout: base.njk
bodyClass: docs-home-page
---
{% from "install.njk" import packageManagers, logoList %}
{#- The specimens below are declared once and used twice: rendered live, and
    highlighted into the source pane beside them. One string, so the markup a
    reader copies is the markup that produced what they are looking at.

    They are written flush to the left margin on purpose: whitespace inside a
    `set` block is captured verbatim, and this is the indentation that ends up
    in the <pre>. -#}

{#- The hero's specimen: the source slab and the rendered card in front of
    it are this one string. Written to fit a phone's code pane without a
    horizontal scrollbar: one attribute set per line, nothing optional. -#}
{%- set heroSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <h2>Sign in</h2>
  <form>
    <label>
      Email
      <input type="email" required>
    </label>
    <label>
      Password
      <input type="password" required>
    </label>
    <label>
      <input type="checkbox" checked>
      Remember me
    </label>
    <button>Sign in</button>
  </form>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- 1. Article + table. Rows on one line, which is how table rows are
    actually written and what keeps the listing to a screenful. -#}
{%- set articleSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header><h3>Team</h3></header>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Role</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Ada Lovelace</th><td>Engineer</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Grace Hopper</th><td>Designer</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <footer><button type="button">Invite</button></footer>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- 2. Disclosure. `name` on a <details> makes the group exclusive, and it
    is the browser doing it. -#}
{%- set detailsSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header><h3>Delivery</h3></header>

  <details name="delivery" open>
    <summary>Standard delivery</summary>
    <p>Three to five working days, tracked. Free.</p>
  </details>
  <details name="delivery">
    <summary>Express delivery</summary>
    <p>Next working day if ordered before 14:00.</p>
  </details>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- 3. Form controls + validation. `type="email"` and `required` are the
    whole validation layer, and `:user-invalid` waits for the reader to
    cause the error. No <form> and no submit on purpose: a form with no
    action would navigate the reader off the home page mid-demo, and the
    only ways to stop that are a script, in the section whose claim is that
    there is none. -#}
{%- set formSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header><h3>Notifications</h3></header>

  <label>
    Email
    <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="you@example.com" required>
  </label>

  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" name="notes" role="switch" checked>
    Send release notes
  </label>

  <button type="button">Save</button>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- The interface the theme section rethemes: a card, a field, an action,
    a floating menu over it and a line of code under it, so a theme moves
    every surface level at once and the depth between them stays. -#}
{%- set themeSpecimen -%}
<article>
  <header>
    <h3>Account settings</h3>
  </header>

  <label>
    Display name
    <input name="name" value="Ada Lovelace">
  </label>

  <details class="dropdown" open>
    <summary>Workspace</summary>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#theme-title">Design</a></li>
      <li><a href="#theme-title">Engineering</a></li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <footer>
    <button type="button">Save changes</button>
    <button type="button" class="secondary">Cancel</button>
  </footer>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- The comparison the demonstration opens with: the same card written with
    a class vocabulary, and as semantic HTML. The first is deliberately
    ordinary, and both counts are taken from the strings, not asserted. -#}
{%- set classSoup -%}
<div class="rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white shadow-sm
            dark:border-neutral-800 dark:bg-neutral-900">
  <div class="border-b border-neutral-200 px-6 py-4
              dark:border-neutral-800">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight
               text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">
      Invite a teammate
    </h3>
  </div>
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 px-6 py-5">
    <label class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium
                  text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
      Email
      <input type="email" required class="h-10 w-full rounded-md
             border border-neutral-300 bg-transparent px-3
             text-sm focus-visible:outline-none
             focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-fuchsia-600
             dark:border-neutral-700">
    </label>
  </div>
  <div class="flex justify-end border-t border-neutral-200
              px-6 py-4 dark:border-neutral-800">
    <button class="inline-flex h-10 items-center rounded-md
                   bg-fuchsia-700 px-4 text-sm font-medium
                   text-white hover:bg-fuchsia-800">
      Send invite
    </button>
  </div>
</div>
{%- endset -%}

{%- set classFree -%}
<article>
  <header>
    <h3>Invite a teammate</h3>
  </header>
  <label>
    Email
    <input type="email" required>
  </label>
  <footer>
    <button>Send invite</button>
  </footer>
</article>
{%- endset -%}

{#- One definition for the three examples, so the tab strip, the panels and
    the source panes cannot disagree about how many there are. -#}
{%- set examples = [
  {
    id: "article",
    tab: "Article",
    name: "Article and table",
    markup: articleSpecimen,
    note: "Nine elements, no classes. <code>&lt;th scope&gt;</code> on both axes gives a screen reader real row and column navigation."
  },
  {
    id: "details",
    tab: "Disclosure",
    name: "Disclosure group",
    markup: detailsSpecimen,
    note: "Open one and the other closes: <code>name</code> makes the group exclusive and the browser runs it. The questions in <a href=\"/why-cirth#before-you-install\">Before you install</a> are this same element."
  },
  {
    id: "form",
    tab: "Form",
    name: "Form and validation",
    markup: formSpecimen,
    note: "Type an address that is not one, then leave the field. <code>required</code> and <code>type=\"email\"</code> are the whole validation layer, and <code>:user-invalid</code> holds the error back until the reader has caused it."
  }
] -%}

{%- set soupCount = classSoup | classCount -%}
{%- set freeCount = classFree | classCount -%}

<main id="main-content" class="docs-home" data-pagefind-body>
	{#- 1. The hero. Copy first in the document and on every screen; the
	    demo is a source slab with the card it renders laid over it, offset
	    so both read at once. The card is rendered by the page's own Cirth,
	    not a screenshot, and is inert: it is a picture of the output, not a
	    form on the home page. The field of colour behind the demo is
	    decoration and nothing else: no text sits on it. -#}
	<section class="docs-hero" aria-labelledby="home-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner docs-hero-grid">
			<div class="docs-hero-copy">
				<h1 id="home-title">Semantic HTML in.<br />Finished UI out.</h1>
				<p class="docs-hero-lede">{{ hero.tagline }}</p>
				<p class="docs-hero-actions">
					{% for action in hero.actions %}
						<a href="{{ action.link }}" role="button"{% if action.theme != "brand" %} class="secondary outline"{% endif %}>{{ action.text }}</a>
					{% endfor %}
				</p>
				<div class="docs-hero-install">
					<p id="hero-install-label">Install from npm</p>
					<pre tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="hero-install-label"><code class="hljs language-sh">npm install @cirthcss/cirth</code></pre>
				</div>
			</div>
			<figure class="docs-hero-demo" aria-labelledby="hero-demo-caption">
				<div class="docs-hero-stack">
					<div class="docs-hero-source">
						<p class="docs-pane-label" aria-hidden="true"><code>sign-in.html</code></p>
						<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Source of the sign-in card"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ heroSpecimen | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
					</div>
					<div class="docs-hero-render" inert>{{ heroSpecimen | safe }}</div>
				</div>
				<figcaption id="hero-demo-caption">The markup on the left is the whole source of the card in front of it. No class, no script.</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- 2. The facts, as one ruled band rather than four boxes. Each is a
	    number from this build or a property the project treats a break of
	    as a bug, and each links to where it can be checked. -#}
	<section class="docs-facts-band" aria-labelledby="facts-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<h2 id="facts-title" class="sr-only">At a glance</h2>
			<dl class="docs-fact-list">
				<div>
					<dt>JavaScript runtime</dt>
					<dd><strong>0 B</strong> <a href="/why-cirth#pure-css-nothing-to-run">Nothing to run</a></dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>Default build, gzipped</dt>
					<dd><strong>{{ proof.size.label if proof.size else "About 15 KB" }}</strong> <a href="/about#size-and-what-it-is-a-budget-for">Measured on every build</a></dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>Accessibility baseline</dt>
					<dd><strong>WCAG 2.2 AA</strong> <a href="/guides/accessibility#the-baseline">How it is audited</a></dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>Frameworks</dt>
					<dd><strong>Any</strong> <a href="/installation#framework-guides">Nine checked guides</a></dd>
				</div>
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- 3. The demonstration. The comparison first, counted from the two
	    strings above; then three real examples on one stage. Every panel is
	    served rendered and unhidden; the tab strip ships `hidden` and the
	    script below turns it on, so without script this is three complete
	    examples under their own headings. -#}
	<section class="docs-demo-section" aria-labelledby="semantic-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="semantic-title">Describe what it is. Cirth draws it.</h2>
				<p>Buttons, fields, tables and disclosures already mean something to the browser, and carry their keyboard behaviour and accessibility with them. Cirth styles those elements directly, so the markup stays the markup you would write anyway.</p>
			</header>
			<figure class="docs-count" aria-labelledby="count-caption">
				<dl class="docs-count-rows" style="--docs-count-max: {{ soupCount }}">
					<div style="--docs-count: {{ soupCount }}">
						<dt>With a utility vocabulary</dt>
						<dd><span class="docs-count-bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ soupCount }} class names</dd>
					</div>
					<div style="--docs-count: {{ freeCount }}">
						<dt>With Cirth</dt>
						<dd><span class="docs-count-bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ freeCount }} class names</dd>
					</div>
				</dl>
				<figcaption id="count-caption">The same invite card, counted.
					<details class="docs-count-source">
						<summary>Show both versions</summary>
						<div class="docs-count-listings">
							<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="The card with utility classes"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ classSoup | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
							<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="The card as semantic HTML"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ classFree | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
						</div>
					</details>
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<div class="docs-switch" data-docs-switch aria-label="Example" hidden>
				{% for example in examples %}
					<button type="button" id="tab-{{ example.id }}" data-docs-tab="{{ example.id }}">{{ example.tab }}</button>
				{% endfor %}
			</div>
			<div class="docs-stage">
				{% for example in examples %}
					<div class="docs-example" id="panel-{{ example.id }}" data-docs-panel="{{ example.id }}">
						<h3 class="docs-example-name">{{ example.name }}</h3>
						<div class="docs-stage-split">
							<div class="docs-stage-code">
								<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-html">{{ example.markup | highlight | safe }}</code></pre>
							</div>
							<div class="docs-stage-preview"><div>{{ example.markup | safe }}</div></div>
						</div>
						<p class="docs-example-note">{{ example.note | safe }}</p>
					</div>
				{% endfor %}
			</div>
			<p class="docs-section-more"><a href="/examples">Browse complete examples</a></p>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- 4. Frameworks. The argument is that Cirth styles what reaches the
	    browser; the marks are where each tool's guide lives. Names are
	    always printed, so each mark is decorative. -#}
	<section class="docs-frameworks" aria-labelledby="frameworks-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner docs-frameworks-grid">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="frameworks-title">Pure CSS. Whatever writes your HTML.</h2>
				<p>Cirth ships no components for any framework and needs none. It styles the HTML that reaches the browser, so a template, a server or a client framework all get the same interface. Every guide was checked in a real project with that tool's own starter.</p>
				<p class="docs-section-more"><a href="/installation#framework-guides">All framework guides</a></p>
			</header>
			<div class="docs-frameworks-marks">
				{{ logoList(frameworks.entries, "home") }}
				<p class="docs-frameworks-note">Logos belong to their projects and say only where a guide lives: none of these projects is affiliated with Cirth or endorses it. <a href="/brand#framework-logos">Sources and terms</a>.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- 5. Themes and depth. The preview carries its own copy of Cirth in a
	    shadow root, so the theme it shows never touches the one the reader
	    picked for the page. Nothing moves on its own: the reader picks a
	    theme and a scheme, and the listing and the preview change together.
	    Without script the preview is the default theme, rendered by the
	    page's own Cirth, and the listing is the default's declarations.
	    The preview is inert, like the hero's card: it is a picture of a
	    themed interface, and its open menu's outside-click catcher is
	    position: fixed, which in a live subtree would cover the page. -#}
	<section class="docs-themes" aria-labelledby="theme-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<header class="docs-section-head">
				<h2 id="theme-title">Three declarations. A different interface.</h2>
				<p>Every colour, radius and font is a <code>--cirth-*</code> custom property. Pick a theme: the markup never changes, and the surfaces keep their depth in light and in dark.</p>
			</header>
			<div class="docs-theme-controls" data-docs-theme-controls hidden>
				<fieldset class="segmented">
					<legend class="sr-only">Theme</legend>
					{% for state in themePreview.states %}
						<label><input type="radio" name="docs-theme-state" value="{{ loop.index0 }}"{% if loop.first %} checked{% endif %}> {{ state.label | replace(" theme", "") | replace(" preset", "") }}</label>
					{% endfor %}
				</fieldset>
				<fieldset class="segmented">
					<legend class="sr-only">Scheme</legend>
					<label><input type="radio" name="docs-theme-scheme" value="light" checked> Light</label>
					<label><input type="radio" name="docs-theme-scheme" value="dark"> Dark</label>
				</fieldset>
			</div>
			<div class="docs-stage docs-theme-stage">
				<div class="docs-stage-split">
					<div class="docs-stage-code">
						<p class="docs-pane-label" data-docs-theme-file><code>{{ themePreview.states[0].file }}</code></p>
						<pre tabindex="0"><code class="hljs language-css" data-docs-theme-block>{{ themePreview.block | safe }}</code></pre>
					</div>
					<div class="docs-stage-preview">
						<cirth-theme-preview data-docs-theme-preview inert>{{ themeSpecimen | safe }}</cirth-theme-preview>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="docs-example-note">Real values, read from the files that declare them: the default theme from <code>cirth.scoped.css</code>, each preset from its own stylesheet. In your own page the same declarations go on <code>:root</code>. <a href="/customization">Every token, and how to override it</a>.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	{#- 6. The close. -#}
	<section class="docs-cta" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
		<div class="docs-home-inner">
			<h2 id="cta-title">Add one stylesheet. Keep your HTML.</h2>
			<p>Start from a CDN link or an npm import. The framework guides cover the rest.</p>
			{{ packageManagers("home") }}
			<p class="docs-hero-actions">
				<a href="/installation" role="button">Read the installation guide</a>
				<a href="/why-cirth" role="button" class="secondary outline">Why Cirth</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</main>


<script>
	// The showcase's tab strip. Everything it needs is already in the page:
	// three rendered examples, each with its own heading, served visible.
	// This turns them into one tab panel at a time and turns the strip on,
	// so a reader without script gets three complete examples rather than a
	// row of buttons that do nothing, and the ARIA roles only ever exist
	// while there is something implementing them.
	(() => {
		const strip = document.querySelector("[data-docs-switch]");
		if (!strip) return;
		const tabs = [...strip.querySelectorAll("[data-docs-tab]")];
		const panels = tabs.map((tab) =>
			document.querySelector(`[data-docs-panel="${tab.dataset.docsTab}"]`),
		);
		if (!tabs.length || panels.some((panel) => !panel)) return;

		strip.setAttribute("role", "tablist");
		for (const [index, tab] of tabs.entries()) {
			const panel = /** @type {HTMLElement} */ (panels[index]);
			tab.setAttribute("role", "tab");
			tab.setAttribute("aria-controls", panel.id);
			panel.setAttribute("role", "tabpanel");
			panel.setAttribute("aria-labelledby", tab.id);
			panel.setAttribute("tabindex", "0");
		}

		/** @param {number} next @param {boolean} moveFocus */
		const select = (next, moveFocus) => {
			for (const [index, tab] of tabs.entries()) {
				const current = index === next;
				tab.setAttribute("aria-selected", String(current));
				// Roving tabindex: one stop for the whole strip, and the arrow
				// keys walk it (WAI-ARIA tabs pattern).
				tab.tabIndex = current ? 0 : -1;
				/** @type {HTMLElement} */ (panels[index]).hidden = !current;
			}
			if (moveFocus) /** @type {HTMLElement} */ (tabs[next]).focus();
		};

		strip.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
			const tab = /** @type {HTMLElement} */ (event.target)?.closest?.(
				"[data-docs-tab]",
			);
			if (tab) select(tabs.indexOf(/** @type {HTMLElement} */ (tab)), false);
		});

		// Selection follows focus: every panel is already in the document, so
		// nothing is fetched or built by arrowing across the strip, and there
		// is no autoplay to take a panel the reader is using away from them.
		strip.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
			const from = tabs.findIndex((tab) => tab === document.activeElement);
			if (from < 0) return;
			const step = { ArrowRight: 1, ArrowDown: 1, ArrowLeft: -1, ArrowUp: -1 }[
				event.key
			];
			const to = step
				? (from + step + tabs.length) % tabs.length
				: event.key === "Home"
					? 0
					: event.key === "End"
						? tabs.length - 1
						: -1;
			if (to < 0) return;
			event.preventDefault();
			select(to, true);
		});

		strip.hidden = false;
		select(0, false);
	})();

	// <cirth-theme-preview>: the theme demo, with its own copy of Cirth.
	//
	// A documentation element, not a Cirth API. What it buys is isolation:
	// the section changes --cirth-* values, the page around it keeps the
	// theme the reader picked in the header, and both are visible at once.
	// A shadow root removes the fight between the two instead of winning it:
	// the page's element rules do not cross the boundary, custom properties
	// inherit in but the scoped build re-declares every one of them on
	// `.cirth`, and the theme is one ordinary stylesheet after Cirth's, which
	// is exactly what the Customization page tells an author to write.
	const ThemePreview = class extends HTMLElement {
		/** @type {HTMLStyleElement | null} */
		#theme = null;
		/** @type {HTMLElement | null} */
		#surface = null;
		/** @type {"light" | "dark" | null} */
		#scheme = null;

		connectedCallback() {
			if (this.shadowRoot) return;
			const root = this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });
			const base = (document.documentElement.dataset.base || "/").replace(
				/\/$/,
				"",
			);

			const cirth = document.createElement("link");
			cirth.rel = "stylesheet";
			cirth.href = `${base}/styles/generated/cirth-lab-scoped.css`;

			// The element's own layout: the part the docs shell owns. The
			// padding lives in here, so the canvas the theme paints is the
			// whole half of the stage and the page's own canvas sits across a
			// hairline from it. The menu is given room to open below its
			// trigger without covering the footer's actions.
			const shell = document.createElement("style");
			shell.textContent = `
				:host { display: grid; }
				.cirth {
					display: grid;
					align-content: center;
					padding: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
					background: var(--cirth-canvas);
				}
				.cirth > article { margin: 0; }
				.cirth { contain: layout paint; }
				.cirth details.dropdown { margin-block-end: 5.5rem; }
				@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
					.cirth, .cirth * {
						transition:
							background-color 0.3s ease,
							border-color 0.3s ease,
							color 0.3s ease,
							border-radius 0.3s ease;
					}
				}
			`;

			const theme = document.createElement("style");
			theme.dataset.cirthTheme = "";

			// The fallback, moved rather than copied: left behind it would be a
			// second, unrendered copy of a form.
			const surface = document.createElement("div");
			surface.className = "cirth";
			surface.append(...this.children);

			root.append(cirth, shell, theme, surface);
			this.#theme = theme;
			this.#surface = surface;

			// Until the reader picks a scheme for the preview, it follows the
			// page's. A shadow tree is styled by its own sheet, so the scheme
			// has to be handed to it: the scoped build reads data-theme off
			// the `.cirth` element itself.
			const follow = () => {
				if (this.#scheme) return;
				surface.dataset.theme =
					document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark"
						? "dark"
						: "light";
			};
			follow();
			new MutationObserver(follow).observe(document.documentElement, {
				attributeFilter: ["data-theme"],
			});
		}

		/** @param {string} css */
		setTheme(css) {
			if (this.#theme) this.#theme.textContent = css;
		}

		/** @param {"light" | "dark"} scheme */
		setScheme(scheme) {
			this.#scheme = scheme;
			if (this.#surface) this.#surface.dataset.theme = scheme;
		}
	};

	if (!customElements.get("cirth-theme-preview")) {
		customElements.define("cirth-theme-preview", ThemePreview);
	}

	// The declarations, on request. Every value is a real one, read out of a
	// compiled file by themePreview() in docs/eleventy.config.js, and the
	// listing and the element are written from the same state in the same
	// step, so the code on the left cannot describe something the interface
	// on the right is not doing.
	(() => {
		const preview = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-preview]");
		const block = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-block]");
		const file = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-file]");
		const controls = document.querySelector("[data-docs-theme-controls]");
		if (!(preview instanceof ThemePreview)) return;
		if (!(block instanceof HTMLElement) || !(controls instanceof HTMLElement)) {
			return;
		}

		const selector = {{ themePreview.selector | dump | safe }};
		const tokens = {{ themePreview.tokens | dump | safe }};
		const states = {{ themePreview.states | dump | safe }};

		/** @param {number} index */
		const apply = (index) => {
			const state = states[index];
			if (!state) return;
			preview.setTheme(
				`${selector} {\n${tokens
					.map((token) => `  ${token}: ${state.lines[token].value};`)
					.join("\n")}\n}`,
			);
			for (const token of tokens) {
				const line = block.querySelector(`.docs-token[data-token="${token}"]`);
				if (line) line.innerHTML = state.lines[token].html;
			}
			if (file) file.innerHTML = `<code>${state.file}</code>`;
		};

		controls.addEventListener("change", (event) => {
			const input = event.target;
			if (!(input instanceof HTMLInputElement)) return;
			if (input.name === "docs-theme-state") apply(Number(input.value));
			if (input.name === "docs-theme-scheme") {
				preview.setScheme(input.value === "dark" ? "dark" : "light");
			}
		});

		// The scheme control starts on the page's own scheme.
		const pageScheme =
			document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark" ? "dark" : "light";
		const schemeInput = controls.querySelector(
			`input[name="docs-theme-scheme"][value="${pageScheme}"]`,
		);
		if (schemeInput instanceof HTMLInputElement) schemeInput.checked = true;

		apply(0);
		controls.hidden = false;
	})();
</script>
